# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# macOS + Apple `container` cannot mount NFS *inside* the container: the Kata
# guest kernel (6.18.15) has no NFS client compiled in -- /proc/filesystems
# lists no `nfs`, there is no /lib/modules to load one from, and
# `mount -t nfs` fails with "failed to prepare mount: No such device".
# --cap-add SYS_ADMIN does not help; it is not a permissions problem, the code
# is simply absent. Bind-mounting a macOS NFS mount through virtiofs is
# unverified and risky (see docs/storage.md).
#
# But the container CAN reach the LAN over its vmnet NAT, egressing as the
# Mac's real IP. So: a Linux host that already has the NFS mount re-serves the
# cache read-only over HTTP, and bitbake consumes it through its own
# officially-supported mirror mechanism. No virtiofs, no NFS-in-guest, no
# kernel surgery.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY NOT `python3 -m http.server`
#
# http.server's own documentation says it is "not recommended for production
# use ... only implements basic security checks". This program deliberately
# does NOT subclass SimpleHTTPRequestHandler. Inheriting it would drag in
# translate_path (which we do not trust), the directory-listing generator
# (which leaks the cache's shape), and a send_head() whose behaviour we would
# have to audit anyway. Everything here is built on BaseHTTPRequestHandler,
# which does request parsing and nothing else, and every filesystem decision
# is made by code in this file that we can defend line by line.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY ONE PROCESS, ONE PORT, MANY ROOTS
#
# The obvious layout is two servers on two ports (sstate on 8100, downloads on
# 8101). We serve both from one process on one port under distinct path
# prefixes instead:
#
#   http://linux-computer:8100/sstate/...
#   http://linux-computer:8100/downloads/...
#
# Because:
#   * One process means one privilege drop, one TLS context, one credential
#     store, one rate limiter, one systemd unit to harden. Two processes means
#     two of each, and two chances to get one of them wrong.
#   * The security-critical code (path resolution) is per-root anyway -- it
#     takes the root as an argument -- so serving N roots costs no extra
#     attack surface, just a dict lookup.
#   * One port is one firewall rule and one TLS certificate.
#   * bitbake does not care: SSTATE_MIRRORS and SOURCE_MIRROR_URL are
#     independent URLs, and a path prefix distinguishes them as well as a port
#     does.
#
# Requires Python 3.7+ (ThreadingHTTPServer landed in 3.7). Standard library
# only, on purpose: it must run on a host with no pip, no venv, no network
# install step -- scp one file and go.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

